    {
      "id": "UgyxAWdoe9ZC9avE4_t4AaABAg",
      "author": "@thezanzibarbarian5729",
      "authorImage": "https://yt3.ggpht.com/ytc/AIdro_mOE_TB7bmFp-HkOXcMLWoBBYd3mwU3N_Fni9jffOyT6KI=s48-c-k-c0x00ffffff-no-rj",
      "text": "I'm watching from England now. But in 1961, I was born in Unguja. Hence my Avatar and the Zanzibari flag.\nWe used to live in a small enclave south of Stone Town called Mazizini. It was mainly British residents with some Americans.\nBut there's not a lot I can remember of the place. We were evacuated in 1964 when the revolution kicked off. So I was only 3 when we left.\nBut what I can remember is, believe it or not, is not Zanzibar, but in Mombasa, Kenya.\nOn the road leading to Mombasa are/were two pairs of man made Elephant tusks that crossed the road. Why I remember those, I don't know. But very weirdly, when we moved to the UK we moved from Plymouth to Sussex and on the dual carriageway into Brighton, the A23, for the first time, I remember looking out for these tusks and not seeing them.\nSo having seen the tusks in Mombasa on that large road, we must have gone there only once. So still being a young kid of 4 or 5 and going to brighton on another large road _(Only the second I'd ever been on. Probably!?)_ I must have thought, _\"Where are those tusks?\"_\nI'd love to return to Zanzibar, but illness would forbid me from doing so. _C'est la vie..._",
      "likeCount": 0,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-30T01:58:55Z",
      "replyCount": 0,
      "replies": []
    },
